---
import PhotoCredit from "../components/PhotoCredit.astro"
import { images } from "../data/images"
---

<section
  class="bg-background-alt px-container-x py-section palette-surface-light"
>
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-landing gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr]">
    <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-28 lg:self-start">
      <figure class="relative m-0">
        <div class="relative w-full overflow-hidden bg-placeholder aspect-3/4">
          <img
            src={images.oldHavana.src}
            alt={images.oldHavana.alt}
            width={images.oldHavana.width}
            height={images.oldHavana.height}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            data-parallax="true"
            class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full scale-110 object-cover photo-tone"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="mt-2">
          <PhotoCredit image={images.oldHavana} />
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="grid gap-16 lg:gap-24">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="mb-4 text-xs font-semibold uppercase text-muted">
          03 — The Streets
        </p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-headline-2 leading-none uppercase">
          Time paused. Life did not.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-copy text-lg text-muted">
          The American cars of the 1950s are not tourist decoration. They are
          real transport: repaired with improvised parts, adapted engines and
          paint carrying decades. Each one is an involuntary monument to the
          shortage of alternatives.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t border-border pt-8">
        <p class="font-display text-headline-3 leading-none uppercase">
          Involuntary Museum
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-copy text-muted">
          The image of the Cuban classic car appears in postcards, documentaries
          and tourism campaigns. The reason is shown less often: decades of
          vehicle import restrictions combined with the need to keep moving.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t border-border pt-8">
        <p class="font-display text-headline-3 leading-none uppercase">
          Mechanical Ingenuity
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-copy text-muted">
          Soviet diesel engines inside Chevrolet bodies. Handmade parts.
          Recycled paint. What the tourist photographs as aesthetic, the driver
          lives as technical survival.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-t border-border pt-8">
        <p class="font-display text-headline-3 leading-none uppercase">
          The Street Speaks
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-copy text-muted">
          Facades, cables, broken asphalt, sidewalks, laundry lines, open
          windows. The Cuban street does not need a script. It needs someone to
          look without the filter of cliche.
        </p>
      </div>
      <blockquote>
        <p
          class="max-w-copy font-serif text-3xl leading-snug text-foreground sm:text-4xl"
        >
          "What the tourist calls charm, the resident calls everyday life."
        </p>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
